        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "58d18071-bcc0-5a1a-9177-09d5175109e5",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Midge Mackenzie, Kate Millett, Betty Friedan, Robert Joffrey\n- Organizations: Joffrey Ballet, BBC, Oxford University, St Hilda's College\n- Products: Astarte, Women Talking, Shoulder to Shoulder\n- Topics: Women's liberation movement, women's suffrage movement\n\nFacts:\n- Midge Mackenzie was born in London on 6 March 1938 and was a writer and filmmaker.\n- Kate Millett attended Oxford University and became the first American woman to receive a degree with first-class honors after studying at St Hilda's College, Oxford.\n- Midge Mackenzie first became known for producing Robert Joffrey's multimedia ballet Astarte with the Joffrey Ballet.\n- Midge Mackenzie also became known for Women Talking, a documentary featuring interviews with Kate Millett, Betty Friedan, and other leading figures in the US women's liberation movement.\n- In 1974, Shoulder to Shoulder was released as a BBC television serial and book about the history of the women's suffrage movement, and both were edited by Midge Mackenzie.\n- Midge Mackenzie died on 28 January 2004.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "f8c48f15-90aa-5e06-bc04-8df05516529d",
        "text": "Page: Midge Mackenzie. Sentence: Margaret Rose MacKenzie, known as Midge Mackenzie, (6 March 1938 - 28 January 2004) was a London-born writer and filmmaker who first become known for producing Robert Joffrey's multimedia ballet \"Astarte\" with the Joffrey Ballet, and \"Women Talking\", a documentary with interviews of Kate Millett, Betty Friedan and other leading figures in the US women’s liberation movement.\nPage: Kate Millett. Sentence: She attended Oxford University and was the first American woman to be awarded a degree with first-class honors after studying at St Hilda's College, Oxford.\nPage: Shoulder to Shoulder. Sentence: Shoulder to Shoulder is a 1974 BBC television serial and book relating the history of the women's suffrage movement, both edited by Midge Mackenzie.",
